Debian 13 independent module qualification: 2026-10-02
====================================================

Copyright 2026 Qore Technologies, s.r.o.

Result: PASS for development snapshots from all 36 independent module
repositories on native amd64 and arm64 (aarch64 Linux on hoppy). This comprises
UUID from QUALIFICATION-DEBIAN13, the original 24-module batch below, the
six-module dependency chain and the final five modules described below.
These are unsigned local packages, not final releases or a published archive.

Qore SDK/runtime: 3.0.0~git20261001.1-0qore1~deb13.
Final evidence bundle: qore-debian13-final-modules-20261001 under the
qualification hosts' temporary directory. It links the earlier checkpoints.
QUALIFICATION.json records exact package versions, source
commits, packaging-overlay hashes and per-architecture reports. QUALIFICATION.txt
summarizes the results. Each module has debian/QUALIFICATION-DEBIAN13, and audits/
records all 71 audit-changes checks. commits.json records module push verification.

Qualified modules
-----------------

amqp, cairo, fsevent, geos, git, imagemagick, kalman, magic, msgpack, mysql,
ncurses, odbc, openldap, pgsql, sqlite3, ssh, ssh2, sybase (freetds), sysconf,
tar, treesitter, vss, zip and zmq. UUID was qualified with the core SDK earlier.
The additional chain qualifies process, xml, python, xmlsec, proj and nats.
The final batch qualifies grpc, jni, pdf, oracle and v8, including combined
installation.
Bundled modules, including JSON and YAML, remain part of Qore's packages.

Original 24-module batch checks
------------------------------

- All 24 source packages reproduce from independent preparations.
- Each module passes two full offline, unprivileged builds on each CPU,
  using different paths and parallelism and varied incoming locale/timezone.
  All 146 first-build binary artifacts reproduce byte-for-byte on their CPU.
- Each module also passes a clean architecture-only (-B) build without
  Doxygen or Graphviz. Its packages match the full builds exactly.
  Totals: 96 full builds and 48 architecture-only builds.
- All 25 architecture-independent packages match across amd64 and arm64.
- Source/binary Lintian has no errors. Binary checking uses the pinned
  Lintian 2.129 checker with the Debian profile in a separate offline image.
  Artifact manifests, ELF hardening, detached debug symbols and Qore AOT
  metadata pass inspection on both CPUs.
- All 96 installed autopkgtest executions pass offline as an unprivileged
  user. Minimal runtime installation without libqore-dev, exact coinstallation
  of documentation/debug packages, dpkg verification, purge, autoremove and
  SDK-free reinstall pass individually for every module on both CPUs.
- Combined installation of 76 artifacts from all 25 repositories passes
  on both CPUs. All 55 native/AOT module names load together in forward and
  reverse order in fresh tiered and AST processes, using only installed /usr
  modules, without the SDK or network access. Installed file verification passes.

Corrections found by qualification
----------------------------------

The ncurses and tar packages produced different AOT objects when build
parallelism changed. Their recipes now fix QCC_JOBS=1 and remove an inherited
QCC_SPLIT_THRESHOLD; independent make targets still build in parallel. Fsevent
receives the same missing reproducibility policy. OpenLDAP's unprivileged
fixture now invokes /usr/sbin/slapd because /usr/sbin is absent from that user's
default PATH. All four corrected source candidates pass the checks above.
Their source manifests preserve the frozen, precommit packaging overlays.

Two issues were in the qualification driver: ImageMagick's own common package
must be available to APT, and SSH/SSH2 tests can consume a driver's piped stdin.
The corrected driver uses a local package index and passes scripts as command
arguments with closed stdin. Original failures and corrected runs are retained.

Additional six-module qualification
-----------------------------------

Evidence bundles: qore-debian13-module-chain-20261001 and
qore-debian13-module-deps-20261001, with the same Qore SDK/runtime as above.
The chain's QUALIFICATION.json records exact module commits, source versions,
packaging overlays, dependency reports, combined installation and audit records.
PROJ includes upstream documentation/CMake fixes from commit 2a36e61eca54.

- All six source packages reproduce independently. 24 full offline builds
  and 12 architecture-only builds pass across both CPUs. All
  36 first-build artifacts reproduce on their CPU; all 6 architecture-
  independent packages also match across CPUs. Architecture-only builds omit
  Doxygen/Graphviz and reproduce the full-build binaries.
- All 26 installed autopkgtest executions pass, including PROJ/Python
  coexistence. Minimal SDK-free installation, exact debug/documentation package
  coinstallation, file verification, purge, autoremove and reinstall pass.
- Source/binary Lintian, ELF hardening, detached debug information and AOT
  metadata checks pass. Each module's qualification file records its scope.
- Combined installation now covers 94 artifacts from all 31 repositories.
  All 82 native/AOT names load together in both orders in fresh tiered and
  AST processes, offline and without the SDK. Installed file verification passes.

Boost 1.90 (1.90.0-6.1~qore4~deb13.1) and NATS C
(3.13.0-2~qore2~deb13.1) add 111 dependency artifacts per CPU. Both backports
pass two full native offline builds, source/binary Lintian, exact binary
reproducibility, hardening/debug inspection and installed tests before and
after purge/autoremove/reinstall. Boost runs all 26 Debian installed-library
tests; NATS exercises pub/sub, request/reply and JetStream revision history.

Existing Debian Boost 1.83 and NATS 3.10 clients remain usable after installing
the newer SDKs, with their original runtimes and loader paths preserved.
Boost 1.83/1.90 filesystem libraries also pass same-process operations in
both load orders. These checks do not establish compatibility for every
possible Boost consumer; the exact tested scope is retained with the evidence.

Qualification caught premature stripping and missing linker hardening in the
Boost.Build bootstrap. The recipe now passes Debian flags to its supported
cxx toolset and lets debhelper split debug symbols. Bootstrap failures propagate;
the redundant unversioned g++ dependency is removed. The CMake generator
uses relative installed paths, and assembly receives debug-prefix mapping,
so the SDK and Boost.Context reproduce across build directories. NATS C uses
an explicit /usr/sbin/nats-server path; the Qore NATS fixtures declare the system PATH for
unprivileged users. The qualification driver also needed XML's separate
libxml2 orig component, prepared by its existing pinned-source repacker. This
required no XML code change. Original failures and corrected candidates are
retained.

Final dependency batch: five completed modules
---------------------------------------------

Evidence bundle: qore-debian13-final-modules-20261001. Its
QUALIFICATION.json records grpc, jni, pdf, oracle and v8, their exact source
commits and source/binary hashes, all 71 audit checks and module push records.
On each CPU, each module passes two full offline builds, exact reproduction,
a third clean architecture-only build, source/binary Lintian, applicable
hardening/debug/AOT checks, installed suites and SDK-free package lifecycle
checks. All architecture-independent module packages match across CPUs.
This adds 20 full builds, 10 architecture-only builds,
36 first-build binary artifacts and 22 installed suite executions.
Final combined integration passes for all 36 repositories on both CPUs:
112 packages and 117 native/AOT module names load together in
forward and reverse order, in fresh tiered and AST processes. Tests run offline
as an unprivileged user using only installed /usr modules, without libqore-dev.
Exact package versions and dpkg file verification pass.
All 40 architecture-independent module packages match across CPUs.

PDFium is rebuilt for Debian ICU 76 on both CPUs. The Ubuntu binary packages
need newer system libraries. The Debian rendering/text-extraction API tests,
reproducibility and lifecycle checks pass. Oracle's Basic/SDK archive contents
remain unchanged; native OCI client/SDK checks pass locally on both CPUs.
Fresh Debian Oracle live checks also pass on both CPUs against a disposable
Oracle AI Database 26ai Free 23.26.2 server with client 23.26.3. Installed
tiered/AST runs each cover 8 suites, 41 executed cases and 169 assertions,
plus fresh compiled clients. The inaccessible legacy system-view LONG case
skips; an independent fixture verifies LONG text larger than 16 KiB.
See oracle-live-20261002/QUALIFICATION.json in the final evidence bundle.
Other server/client combinations and optional server-restart/AQ demos are
outside this result. The temporary database and credentials were removed.
JNI passes runtime/compiler/Kotlin suites with the approved pinned-JAR policy.
Its private JVM RUNPATH is restricted to the exact packaged OpenJDK 21 library.

Ten additional source packages pass two native offline builds per CPU,
exact binary reproducibility, source/binary Lintian, applicable hardening/debug
checks, installed tests and purge/autoremove/reinstall with repeated tests:
Cython, PyArrow, simdjson, simdutf, Ada URL, libuv, Acorn, llhttp, TypeScript
and Rollup Buble. Their portable Architecture: all packages match across CPUs.
PyArrow uses system Arrow 25 libraries; array/compute/IPC, dataset/Parquet,
Acero and loopback Flight checks pass, followed by gRPC's Flight interoperability
suite. This is not a claim of full upstream PyArrow test coverage.

Qualification corrected missing hardening flags, random Cython build paths,
absolute paths in Ada documentation, dh-nodejs watch-v5 component discovery,
and llhttp test typings. llhttp's native-library/source scope avoids replacing
Debian's JavaScript generator package. Its portable source manifest excludes
host-specific pkg-config output, and its generated sources match the bootstrap
on both CPUs. See debian/backports/README and the dependency evidence reports.

Node 22 and V8 qualification
---------------------------

Node 22.22.2 (3~qore4~deb13.1) passes independent source preparation, two
native offline builds per CPU, exact binary reproducibility, source/binary
Lintian, strict hardening/debug inspection, installed tests and full
purge/autoremove/reinstall with repeated tests. Its portable packages match
across CPUs. Each of the four test runs per CPU executes more than 3,000
cases from the retained Debian message/parallel/sequential suites. The pinned
Debian skip lists and flaky-test policy remain unchanged, including known
HTTP2 timeouts; there are no unquarantined failures. Exact counts, skips and
quarantined failures are recorded in dependencies/<architecture>/nodejs/
test-policy.json. This is not a claim of full upstream Node test coverage.

The Node recipe resolves minimatch's installed entry point, imports Debian
build flags before exporting custom flags, and removes the build-tree RUNPATH
from the staged executable before debhelper splits debug symbols. Build-tree
execution still uses its original RUNPATH. DWZ reaches its upstream-configured
optimization limit for the large Node library; detached debug information
remains complete and passes the strict artifact checks.

V8 uses that Node SDK and the separately qualified TypeScript 5.2 backport.
Its four packages reproduce on each CPU; portable packages match across CPUs.
Qualification corrected the schema-cache test's child-process module path:
it honors the existing packaging qmod override while preserving the source
fallback. Both modes pass all seven cache cases and 175 assertions.
Installed tests retain eight source-only proxy fault-injection skips and two
optional HubSpot catalogue fixture skips.
See module-v8 commit 947892c7 and the final batch's exact source manifest.

The combined check also found Node initialization replacing Qore's signal
mask/handlers and the JVM's SIGSEGV handler. V8 now requests Node's supported
kNoDefaultSignalHandling embedding behavior. An isolated SIGTERM regression
fails before the fix and passes afterward, WebAssembly bounds errors remain
correct, and all 117 native/AOT names load together in both orders and modes.
See module-v8 commit e093a183 and the recorded signal-fix audit.
Its normal CI pipeline 58525 passes both Ubuntu and Alpine jobs.

OBS output qualification checkpoint: October 3
----------------------------------------------

Evidence bundle: qore-obs-debian13-20261002/followup-20261003.
MODULE-OUTPUT-QUALIFICATION.json accepts 34 independent module sources and
105 binary packages per CPU against the actual corrected OBS Qore/SDK
3.0.0~git20261001.1-0qore2~deb13. This checkpoint excludes V8, whose OBS
Node dependency is still building, and Oracle, whose hosting approval is
pending. It does not replace the earlier local qualification of those modules.

The 32-module installed batch passes 66 autopkgtests per CPU, minimum runtime
installation without the SDK, imports, file verification, purge/autoremove
and reinstallation. SSH and UUID have separate accepted installed results.
For 18 module sources, prior reproduced payloads and metadata are unchanged;
only explicitly checked Qore dependency/Built-Using controls may differ.
Thirteen other sources reproduce the OBS binaries independently against the
actual OBS SDK. JNI required the correction described below. These are
distinct forms of evidence; unchanged payload comparisons do not claim a
new byte-for-byte rebuild of the changed control archive.

JNI's make-inc embedded Java classes in filesystem traversal order. Sorting
directory/class inputs retains inner-class precedence and preserves all 3,048
decompressed Byte Buddy class payloads. The regression fails before the fix
and passes afterward on both CPUs. Module commit 3186bd5e2833 implements the
fix and adds it to Ubuntu/Alpine CI; both jobs pass with refreshed base images.
The backport retains the frozen module source and original vendor archives.
Corrected version 2.7.0~git20261001.1+ds-0qore2~deb13 passes two identical
native builds per CPU, all eight CTests, strict artifact/Lintian checks and
runtime/compiler/Kotlin package tests. All five OBS outputs per CPU match
those qualified binaries exactly. APT upgrades from the .1 candidate to .2
also pass all three installed suites and file verification on both CPUs.

SSH's upstream fixture fix 4a45cf2fc87d is backported into its .2 candidate;
the test now creates its own inside/outside files instead of assuming that
/etc/hostname exists in the build environment. Both OBS builds match the
independently qualified native packages exactly. All 60 OBS source inventories
match local imports, and publication remains disabled. The previous combined
34-module test predates JNI .2; refresh it with JNI .2 and V8 before publication.

Remaining release gates
-----------------------

Oracle dependency hosting approval and the optional V8 app catalogue remain
separate gates. This checkpoint covers the native V8 bridge packaging plan,
not the separately built app catalogue.

No prior Debian upgrade/ABI baseline is available. Only the service scenarios
exercised by package tests are qualified. Final ABI/release freeze, acceptance
of exact release artifacts, dependency maintenance and durable evidence
retention remain required. Public Debian delivery needs a signed Debian archive;
an Ubuntu PPA does not publish these packages.
